{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:01:06Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 16, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "--- git status ---\nOn branch feature/tp-4/give-tasks-priority\nChanges not staged for commit:\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to update what will be committed)\n  (use \"git restore <file>...\" to discard changes in working directory)\n\tmodified:   docs/design.md\n\nno changes added to commit (use \"git add\" and/or \"git commit -a\")\n--- docs/spec.md ---\n# Spec: Edit a task title\n\nTaiga #3\n\n## Triage\n\n**Feature** \u2014 net-new capability. Touches both `backend/` and `frontend/`,\nbut stays a single small feature: one new endpoint, one new store method, one\nnew interaction on an existing list row. Not split into sub-specs \u2014 the\nlayers here are the normal shape of a full-stack change in this app, not a\ndisproportionate one (compare Taiga #1, which was frontend-only because\nfiltering needed no server change; this one legitimately needs a write\nendpoint because the title itself is server state).\n\n## Goal\n\nLet someone fix a mistyped task title in place, without deleting and\nre-typing the whole task.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Given a task in the list, when the user activates edit mode for its title\n   (an explicit \"Edit\" trigger, exact treatment the ux-designer's call), then\n   the title becomes an editable input pre-filled with the task's current\n   title.\n2. Given a task in edit mode, when the user changes the text and confirms\n   (Save), then the task's title is updated both via a backend call and in\n   the UI, and edit mode closes showing the new title.\n3. Given a task in edit mode, when the user cancels, then edit mode closes,\n   the title reverts to its original value, and **no backend call is made**.\n4. Given a task in edit mode, when the user submits with an empty title,\n   then the edit is rejected: the task's title is **not** changed, no\n   backend call that would wipe the title succeeds, and the user sees that\n   the edit didn't go through (exact feedback \u2014 inline message vs. disabled\n   Save \u2014 is the ux-designer's call, mirroring how `AddTask` disables `Add`\n   until there's a trimmed, non-empty value).\n5. Given a task in edit mode, when the user submits a title that is only\n   whitespace, then it is treated the same as empty (criterion 4) \u2014 trimmed\n   before the empty check, mirroring `AddTask.jsx`'s existing\n   `title.trim()` guard.\n6. Backend: a new endpoint accepts a title update for an existing task,\n   reuses the existing `TaskIn` validation (`min_length=1, max_length=200`),\n   and returns the updated `Task`.\n7. Given the task id in the update request does not exist, then the backend\n   returns `404`, matching the existing `complete_task`/`delete_task`\n   pattern in `backend/app/main.py`.\n8. Given a title update request with an empty string, then the backend\n   returns `422` via the existing `TaskIn` field validation \u2014 same\n   behaviour as `create_task` today (`test_empty_title_is_rejected` already\n   covers the create path; the new route needs its own equivalent test).\n--- docs/design.md exists? ---\nBACKLOG.md\ndesign.md\nspec.md", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_018QYqY9YWck5EPq5zqbHMv5", "session_id": "96438b12-b321-4900-8b09-8b900d83292c"}}
